<p>A <strong>relational database</strong> stores facts in tables and connects them through shared values. It answers questions across those tables, refuses changes that break specified rules and can make several changes succeed or fail together. Those abilities are useful when several people or processes depend on the same facts.</p>
<p>Consider a shop. Mina orders a mug and two packets of tea. The shop needs to remember who ordered, what each item cost, how much stock remains and whether payment succeeded. Looking up an order is one job. Keeping those facts correct while two people buy the last mug is another.</p>
<p>We will follow the shop from its first tables to the decisions involved in running it. PostgreSQL supplies the concrete SQL and transaction behaviour; other relational engines share the broad ideas but differ in details that matter. The <a href="#choose-a-system-by-testing-the-contract-you-need">reading routes at the end</a> continue from this introduction. Each main article completes a working case before its deeper links take up particular questions.</p>

<nav aria-label="In this article" class="article-contents"><ol>{sections.map(([id, title]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="one-action-changes-several-facts">One order, several changes</h2>
<p>Suppose there is one mug left. Accepting a new order must record the order, record its mug item and reduce available stock from one to zero. If the application saves those changes separately and stops after the second, the order has an item but the mug is still available to sell. Reordering the writes changes which partial result can remain.</p>
<p>The database gives these shared facts one place to be read and changed. All the shop’s application instances use it, and it coordinates their competing writes. A <strong>transaction</strong> lets the application group the three changes so that their committed effect is all or nothing.</p>
<p>The shop still has to say what is correct. “Available stock cannot be negative” is a rule about one stored value. “Every accepted order has reserved its items” also depends on how orders and reservations are changed together. The database can check rules we express; it cannot infer this second rule from a table called <code>orders</code>.</p>
<p>Payment belongs to a separate system. The shop can store a payment attempt, but rolling back its database cannot undo a charge at the provider. The order needs states such as waiting for payment, paid and cancelled, with a next action for an interrupted or declined attempt. We will return to that boundary after storing and querying the order itself.</p>

<h2 id="tables-describe-facts-and-relationships">Tables, keys and relationships</h2>
<p>A <strong>table</strong> holds rows with named, typed <strong>columns</strong>. Each <strong>row</strong> represents one instance of a fact: one customer, one order or one product within an order. Say what that instance means before choosing its columns.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The shop’s four tables"><table><caption>Four kinds of fact in the shop</caption><thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>One row represents</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td><code>customers</code></td><td>A customer’s current details</td><td><code>customer_id = 1</code>, <code>name = 'Mina'</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>orders</code></td><td>An order belonging to a customer</td><td><code>order_id = 101</code>, <code>customer_id = 1</code>, <code>status = 'paid'</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>order_items</code></td><td>One product’s quantity and purchased price on an order</td><td><code>order_id = 101</code>, <code>product_id = 'TEA'</code>, <code>quantity = 2</code>, <code>unit_price_gbp = 8.00</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>products</code></td><td>A product and its available stock</td><td><code>product_id = 'MUG'</code>, <code>available = 1</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>A <strong>primary key</strong> uniquely identifies a row and cannot be missing. Two customers can share a name, and Mina can change hers, so the shop gives each customer an integer identifier. Order 101 stores customer 1’s identifier. Correcting Mina’s current name then changes one customer row rather than a copy on every order.</p>
<p>The order’s <code>customer_id</code> is a required <strong>foreign key</strong>: it must identify an existing customer. An order for customer 999 is refused if no such customer exists. A customer may still have no orders. Each item similarly references an order and a product. Its primary key is <code>(order_id, product_id)</code>, a pair, because this shop combines the same product into one quantity per order. Separate discount lines would need a different identity.</p>
<p>The <strong>schema</strong> defines these tables, types and rules. Quantity is an integer, required with <code>NOT NULL</code> and positive with <code>CHECK (quantity &gt; 0)</code>. The price is pounds to two decimal places, stored here as exact decimal <code>numeric(10,2)</code>. It records the purchased price: tomorrow’s catalogue change must not rewrite yesterday’s agreement.</p>
<p><code>NULL</code> is SQL’s marker for a missing value. An order’s <code>shipped_at</code> can be NULL until shipment is recorded. It is neither a date nor the text “NULL”. If the shop must distinguish “not shipped” from “shipment time unknown”, it needs to represent that distinction too.</p>
<p>These are logical choices about facts. Files, pages and indexes are how the engine stores and finds them. Changing an index can make an order cheaper to find without changing what it means. Separating customer and order facts also leaves them available together through a join, which we will use next.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/data-design/">Designing relational data</a> builds and tests a complete schema from requirements, including the rules it cannot enforce. Its deeper articles develop values, constraints, normalisation and history.</p>

<h2 id="ask-for-a-result-then-check-what-it-means">Asking questions with SQL</h2>
<p><strong>SQL</strong> is the language most relational systems use to define, read and change data. A <code>SELECT</code> asks for rows. This one asks for Mina’s order identifiers and statuses:</p>
<Sql code={selectOrders} label="Mina’s orders, in identifier order" />
<p><code>FROM</code> names the input table, <code>WHERE</code> keeps rows meeting the condition and <code>SELECT</code> chooses the output columns. Our small example contains Mina’s paid order 101 and Arun’s empty draft 102, so the result is one row: <code>101, paid</code>. <code>ORDER BY</code> requests the result order. Without it, an order you happen to observe is not promised.</p>
<p>A <strong>join</strong> pairs rows with matching values. Join each order to its customer and items, then use <code>GROUP BY</code> to gather the matches for each order. <code>SUM</code> adds quantity times purchased price:</p>
<Sql code={summary} label="An item total for each order with items" />
<p>The short names <code>o</code>, <code>c</code> and <code>i</code> are table aliases. Figure 1 starts after matching customers, so the names are already present. Predict how many rows order 101 will produce when it meets its two items, then step through. At the last step, choose <em>Keep: LEFT JOIN</em> and compare Arun’s order.</p>
<RowsFigure />
<p>Order 101 appeared twice, once per item, before grouping made one £36 total. Counting those joined rows would count item matches, not orders. Both rows are legitimate: each represents a different order–item pair. Decide what one output row should mean before trying to remove repeats.</p>
<p>The ordinary join omitted order 102 because it had no item. A <code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps an unmatched order with NULLs on the item side. Its sum is NULL because there are no item values to add. Displaying zero is a separate decision. <code>COUNT(*)</code> would count the retained row as one; <code>COUNT(i.product_id)</code> counts non-null product values and gives zero.</p>
<p>Missing values affect filters too. <code>shipped_at = NULL</code> produces an unknown comparison, not a missing-value test. Use <code>shipped_at IS NULL</code>. A <code>WHERE</code> keeps only true conditions, so the wrong test silently excludes the rows you wanted.</p>
<p>SQL describes the requested result; the engine chooses how to find it. Neither a successful query nor an attractive sample proves that the question was right. The same applies to <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code>: a valid statement can change the wrong orders. Check an expected answer on a tiny dataset, including empty orders and repeated matches, before trusting it on the shop’s data.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/sql/">Working with SQL</a> supplies a disposable starting database and carries a report from its first query to a checked answer, followed by pagination and changes.</p>

<h2 id="a-transaction-protects-a-change-within-a-stated-contract">Transactions and competing buyers</h2>
<p>Return to the last mug and a new order, 103. Put the order insertion, item insertion and stock reduction between <code>BEGIN</code> and <code>COMMIT</code>. In PostgreSQL, that transaction sees its own unfinished changes; ordinary readers in other transactions do not. <code>COMMIT</code> keeps the changes together. <code>ROLLBACK</code> discards them together.</p>
<p>Play Figure 2 with separate commits and a failed request. At the last step, choose <em>One transaction</em> and compare what remains. Then choose <em>Completes</em>, reset and watch when the new reader sees the stock fall.</p>
<OrderFigure />
<p>Separate commits left an order and item without a stock reservation. Rolling back one transaction removed both new rows. On success, commit made all three changes available together. This all-or-nothing property is <strong>atomicity</strong>.</p>
<p>Now Mina and Arun both try to buy the mug. Each can read one available before either commits. If both calculate zero in application memory and later write that value, stock can finish at zero while two orders are accepted. Each transaction can be atomic and the combined result still be wrong. <strong>Isolation</strong> defines which interactions between overlapping transactions the engine permits.</p>
<p>For this one-row rule, PostgreSQL can test and reduce stock in one statement:</p>
<Sql code={reserve} label="Reserve one mug only while stock remains" />
<p>At PostgreSQL’s default <em>Read Committed</em> isolation, a competing updater waits for this row’s writer, then rechecks the condition against its committed version. Once the first buyer takes stock to zero, the second gets no returned row. The application must treat that as no reservation and roll back its attempted order. It commits the order and item only when one unit was reserved. A non-negative stock check is an extra guard; checking the statement’s result remains necessary.</p>
<p>That settles one counter. Rules over several rows need an argument about constraints, locks or stronger isolation that covers every writer. <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a> develops those choices and tests competing sessions. The initials <strong>ACID</strong> collect atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability. Consistency means preserving the system’s rules; the initials cannot supply missing rules or correct a mistaken change.</p>
<p><strong>Durability</strong> is the commit’s survival promise. PostgreSQL normally waits for its local write-ahead log to be flushed so committed changes can be reconstructed after a crash. Asynchronous commit can reply earlier and lose recent commits in a crash. A durable local commit still cannot protect a destroyed storage device. Establish the engine, settings and failures covered before relying on a success reply.</p>
<p>The payment provider remains outside this transaction. One useful design commits the reservation with a durable instruction to request payment, then lets a worker perform that work. The record makes unfinished work discoverable; a provider’s declined payment or lost reply still needs a recovery decision.</p>

<h2 id="the-application-is-part-of-the-database-system">From a request to a committed order</h2>
<p>A <strong>driver</strong> sends the application’s statements and translates returned values. For a database service, it uses a connection to the server; an embedded engine runs within the application process.</p>
<p>Supply Mina’s identifier as a bound value. PostgreSQL statement text can say <code>WHERE customer_id = $1</code>, with the integer passed separately through the driver’s binding API. Her input then remains a value rather than becoming SQL syntax. The application must also establish that the signed-in user may read customer 1’s orders. Binding an identifier does not authorise it.</p>
<p>A server application usually borrows a connection from a bounded <strong>pool</strong>. Its transaction must stay on that connection until commit or rollback; sending <code>BEGIN</code> on one connection and the updates on another does not group them. Finish and clean up the transaction before returning the connection, and keep unrelated network calls outside its lifetime.</p>
<p>A database rejection and a broken connection carry different evidence. If the database rejects the change because it violates a constraint, that change failed. A connection lost during commit may conceal success. Give each purchase attempt an identifier, such as <code>R103</code>, separate from the customer and order identifiers. Reuse that purchase-attempt identifier for every retry, and store it with the order under a uniqueness rule, together with its result. Step through Figure 3, then switch the failure point at the last step. Compare the application’s knowledge with the stored order.</p>
<DecisionFigure scene="outcome" number={3} />
<p>The same connection error can accompany a committed order or no order. Retrying the same purchase with a new purchase-attempt identifier can create a second order. Use the original purchase-attempt identifier, R103, to recover its recorded result or coordinate another attempt. A lookup that finds nothing must also account for an overlapping attempt still able to commit; it is not permission for an uncoordinated retry.</p>
<p>Give the payment request a stable payment-request identifier too, and reuse it for retries where the provider supports duplicate prevention. Reconcile uncertain external outcomes. A timeout limits the caller’s wait; it does not prove remote work stopped. Bound retries and connection demand together, because immediate retries can deepen an overload. <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a> follows the complete request, including value conversion, connection cleanup and recoverable external work.</p>

<h2 id="correct-sql-still-has-to-do-physical-work">The work behind a query</h2>
<p>Returning one order can mean testing every row in a table. An <strong>index</strong> gives the engine a searchable route from a value to matching rows. A primary key commonly supplies one; an index on <code>orders.customer_id</code> may help find Mina’s orders as the table grows.</p>
<p>Engines store several rows together in blocks called <strong>pages</strong>. Fetching one row can mean reading its whole page. Figure 4 uses twelve invented orders to keep both routes visible. Step through the scan, then switch to <em>Searching an index</em>. Compare the table pages examined, as well as the returned order.</p>
<DecisionFigure scene="lookup" number={4} />
<p>The index saved table work for one match. A request for nearly every order may reach most pages anyway and be cheaper as a scan. Each index also takes space and adds work to changes that maintain it. Joins, sorting and sending large results consume resources even when the final answer contains few rows.</p>
<p>A <strong>query plan</strong> shows the route the engine chose. PostgreSQL’s <code>EXPLAIN</code> reports estimates; <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> executes the statement and adds observed rows and page accesses. It performs changes too, so collect execution evidence for changing statements in a suitable disposable environment.</p>
<p>For a slow order-history page, record its SQL, parameters, data size and delay. Find where the time goes: waiting for a connection, executing or transferring the answer. If execution dominates, inspect the measured plan. Reading thousands of rows to return ten supports investigating the access path; a long lock wait raises a different question. Predict what one change should remove, compare the same correct result before and after, then check writes and concurrent requests too.</p>
<p>Requests share CPU, memory, storage, locks and connections. Averages can hide the slowest responses, so compare latency percentiles and completed requests per second under realistic competing work. <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a> completes that diagnosis and comparison. Its evidence then leads to <a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">query-plan diagnosis</a> for expensive execution or <a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/">Improving performance under concurrent load</a> for queues and contention.</p>

<h2 id="data-and-applications-change-at-different-speeds">Changing a live database</h2>
<p>The shop now requires a delivery instruction on submitted orders. Existing orders have no delivery instruction, and older application instances never send one. Filling every old row with an empty string may satisfy a rule while concealing that the information was never collected.</p>
<p>In Figure 5, compare requiring the value immediately with waiting until the writers and affected rows are ready. Follow what happens to the old application’s next order.</p>
<DecisionFigure scene="change" number={5} />
<p>Add the delivery-instruction column as optional first. Deploy compatible writers, convert existing data only where a valid value can be established, then verify the rows and retire old writers before strengthening the rule. The final rule may need to distinguish historical orders from new ones. A deployment is not finished while an old worker can still write the old format.</p>
<p>The database has physical work to do as well: a schema change may scan or rewrite rows and hold locks. Rehearse on representative data, allow spare capacity and give a long conversion bounded batches and recorded progress. Decide how to stop or resume it. Reverting code or recreating a dropped column does not bring back lost values.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> carries one representation change through compatibility, conversion, verification and retirement, then extends the method to moving a database.</p>

<h2 id="operate-for-the-failure-you-must-recover-from">Keeping the service and its data</h2>
<p>The shop needs the database to remain usable and its answers to remain trustworthy. Give ordinary requests only the access they need, protect credentials and network access, and name the people responsible for maintenance and recovery. Watch errors and latency alongside connections, waits and storage growth. Capacity must leave room for a surge, an index build or a recovery, as well as today’s traffic.</p>
<p>A <strong>replica</strong> is another copy receiving changes. It may help replace a failed writer, but whether acknowledged orders survive depends on its progress and what commit waited for. Its reads may also lag. A <strong>backup</strong> preserves recoverable data from an earlier state. With suitable retained change history, the engine may recover to a point before a mistake.</p>
<p>Figure 6 starts with order 101 on both current copies. Compare losing the writer with committing a deletion. Watch whether promoting the replica could recover the order.</p>
<DecisionFigure scene="recovery" number={6} />
<p>Replication copied the mistaken deletion. Retained earlier state answers that failure, but returning the whole database to that earlier moment would also omit valid later orders. Restore separately, validate the affected relationships and decide how to reconcile them with current work before changing the live service.</p>
<p>State acceptable loss and interruption for each failure. The allowed loss is the <strong>recovery point objective</strong>; the allowed recovery duration is the <strong>recovery time objective</strong>. A successful backup job does not establish either. Time an isolated restore and application checks, including the keys, configuration and capacity needed to use it. For the shop, check orders with their items, stock reservations and payment references. A running server with wrong orders has not completed recovery.</p>
<p>A managed service takes on specified duties; the team still needs to check its retention and restore limits, remaining responsibilities and recovery access. <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a> connects those requirements to access, monitoring, maintenance and incident decisions before its detailed procedures.</p>

<h2 id="choose-a-system-by-testing-the-contract-you-need">Choosing a database and a reading route</h2>
<p>The shop has related facts, integrity rules, changes that belong together and questions that will evolve. Those make a relational system a useful candidate. Specialised search or analytical work may justify another store, with additional synchronisation and recovery costs. Begin with the work and promises rather than the product name.</p>
<p>An embedded library such as SQLite runs with the application. A server such as PostgreSQL accepts client connections. A distributed relational service coordinates data across machines. Shared SQL does not give them identical write concurrency, transaction behaviour or recovery. The engine, its arrangement of copies and who operates it are separate choices.</p>
<p>Write a short selection brief: the stock rule, peak competing orders, response-time target, data growth, acceptable loss and interruption, and the team’s operating capacity. Turn hard requirements into rejection tests. Exercise candidates with the same schema and competing work through the intended driver and network, and include recovery and operating cost. <a href="/review/relational-next/choosing/">Choosing a relational database</a> develops that evaluation and keeps a PostgreSQL candidate record. Take its <a href="/review/relational-next/sqlite/">SQLite follow-up</a> for an embedded candidate, or its <a href="/review/relational-next/cockroachdb/">CockroachDB follow-up</a> when a concrete distributed requirement survives comparison with a simpler service.</p>
<p>You can begin learning before that choice is complete. Use the disposable PostgreSQL setup at the start of <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/">Working with SQL</a> for practice, then choose the route matching your job.</p>
